A||||8||dantheman1998||450 hp? will trans hold up?||||1||
Z||000000||dantheman1998||10-23-2001||10:07 PM||||i have a 450 hp engine that i am going to bie<BR>. now all i need is a fiero. what fiero will hold up to this power? will the th-125 hold power?i have heard of a 125 -c what is that?<BR>the trans has to hold up to 1/4 racing. do they make torque converters for this trans?what is the best year fiero and model for the lowest amount of money?||||reg||1
Z||000001||dennis_6||10-23-2001||10:10 PM||||No, The TH-125 Automatic will not hold that power.||||reg||1
Z||000002||AkursedX||10-23-2001||10:24 PM||||Yes, the th-125 auto will hold that kind of power IF it is built properly.  Archie knows of a place the will "beef" up your auto for either street or strip applications.  I'm guessing it runs around 1000 dollars to do.  But, like with anything, the added abuse will most likely shorten it's life.<P>I think the Getrag 5-speed would probably hold up to that power, the problem is finding a clutch that'll hold it.  <P>But for 1/4 mile runs, i would go with the auto, but that's just my opinion.<P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bazooka||10-23-2001||11:25 PM||||Dantheman,    Speaking from experience, the 125c tranny will most definately NOT stand up to that kind of power. I had less HP than that and the experts even built and installed my 125c and it still wouldn't last, rebuild after rebuild. Don't waste your money, shoot for a predictable 215 HP. <BR>*************************<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by dantheman1998: i have a 450 hp engine that i am going to bie<BR>. now all i need is a fiero. what fiero will hold up to this power? will the th-125 hold power?<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>***************************<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||California Kid||10-24-2001||12:20 AM||||The Getrag will hold the power, but your going to have to go with a QuarterMaster Double Disk Clutch Setup. If you have an machining talent on spline shafts, you could pull the shaft on the trans and "chase" the splines down about another 1/4 inch, then you can package 3 Clutch Disks with 2 Floater Plates.<P>The QuarterMaster setup is a "Race Only" application, but I have been running in on the street with a 400hp Corvette L98. Check their web site out. I currently have approx. 10,000 miles on my twin disks with absolutely no slip in any gear. I expect to have to change them sometime next season, the special organic friction material is very thin on race clutches (so they don't hold heat), so you won't get real long life like standard clutches.<BR>Just my opinion.<BR>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Signature_7.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by California Kid (edited 10-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||artherd||10-24-2001||12:48 AM||||No way dude, the TH125 will probally just explode.  I mean, bloody CHAIN drive?!<P>You'll be best off finding a (much) beefier Transverse mounting auto tranny to match your new engine (you didn't say what kind, but unless you're insane, it just about has to be a 350 chevy V8)<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by dantheman1998:<BR><B>i have a 450 hp engine that i am going to bie<BR>. now all i need is a fiero. what fiero will hold up to this power? will the th-125 hold power?i have heard of a 125 -c what is that?<BR>the trans has to hold up to 1/4 racing. do they make torque converters for this trans?what is the best year fiero and model for the lowest amount of money?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000006||Turbo88||10-24-2001||12:54 AM||||So the consensus is that the Getrag will hold 450hp from a v8?||||reg||1
Z||000007||SKIDMARK||10-24-2001||01:10 AM||||No, I don't think you will find an automatic transaxle to fit the Fiero that will hold up to that kind of torture....at least for more than a couple of 1/4 mile runs. Besides, the Fiero isn't designed to be a drag car, It's all about cornering! <P>------------------<BR>White '85 4cyl coupe<BR>Black '86 v-6 SE<BR>'81 CB900F Super Sport||||reg||13
Z||000008||California Kid||10-24-2001||06:48 AM||||"skidmark" makes an excellent point about the design intent of the car. If anyone really wants a Fiero to be a "Drag Only" car, put the engine up front, add a strong rear axle, and tub out the rear wheelhouses for some huge slicks.  Have seen a few done and run at the drags, very impressive, neat looking drag cars. My trips to the strip were curiosity only, on what it would do, not a hobby.||||reg||1
